{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 15, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering drug decriminalization experiment is set to end on Sunday as a new law comes into effect, re-criminalizing possession of small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, offers those caught with illicit substances a choice between being charged with possession or undergoing treatment, which involves completing a behavioral health program.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option includes participating in a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines, and personal-use possession will be punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to make it easier for police to tackle public drug use and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near certain areas. Backers argue that it allows counties to develop tailored programs based on their resources.\n\n*** \nHowever, opponents are concerned that this may create an inconsistent and unfair policy landscape. Portland police chief Bob Day highlighted the narrow criteria for deflection, including no other charges, no warrants, and no violent behavior. If a person meets these requirements, officers will call a dispatch line to connect them to a mobile behavioral health team.\n\n*** \nAs the country watches Oregon's approach, it's worth considering the broader implications. At a certain age, people may be more likely to eat into their savings for shopping sprees, but the tongue of lawmakers is often sharp when it comes to budget decisions. P.P.S As the debate continues, it's clear that the issue is complex, and the country will be watching to see how Oregon's new law plays out, with some wondering if it will leave a bitter taste on the tongue of those affected.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -9.059865078597795e-06}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.4740769863128662